[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated the suspected flapping of router4’s LAN interface (192.168.200.1 on eth0), which is the default gateway for Location C (192.168.200.0/24). Multiple on-demand tests from all affected PCs (PC11–PC14) to 192.168.200.1 showed 0% packet loss and sub‑1 ms RTT over repeated runs (10–40 pings), indicating no dropouts at the time of testing. On router4, eth0 is UP/LOWER_UP with 192.168.200.1/24, zero RX/TX errors or drops, and ethtool reports link detected: yes, 10G full duplex. Kernel logs (dmesg) show no carrier up/down events for eth0. 

On the L2 path, switch4 (uplink to router4) and access switches switch5/switch6 (to PC11–14) have all relevant ports in forwarding state with no errors or flaps observed; the bridge/FDB tables show router4’s MAC consistently learned on the correct port. ARP/neighbor tables on both PCs and router4 are stable, with expected STALE->REACHABLE refresh behavior and no signs of churn.

Mechanism check: if eth0 were flapping or overloaded, we would see link up/down events, interface errors/drops, ARP churn, and concurrent ICMP loss—all absent here. Localization: router4.eth0 <-> switch4.eth0 and downstream access links are stable. Root cause: No fault found on the gateway; the reported “dropouts” were not reproduced and are unlikely due to router4’s LAN interface. Corrective fix: None required on router4. Recommend client-side checks (NIC power management/cabling), and, if the issue recurs, deploy continuous monitoring (e.g., smokeping to 192.168.200.1 and link-state syslog/SNMP traps) to capture any intermittent events. 
=== END ===